// ===========================================================================
// OBESITY — LAYERED. Layering NAMES REDUCTIONS. It says nothing about answering.
//
// ⭐ ONE OF THREE LEGITIMATE AUTHORING OPTIONS (operator, 2026-08-29), and the
// one we expect to establish as the convention. Assume an Obesity LIBRARY whose
// every layer some other library consumes.
//
// ⭐ WHAT LAYERING IS FOR: one history, several NAMED reductions over it, each
// stated once and reusable by another library. `Most Recent Weight` and
// `Greatest Weight` both read `Weight Records`, and neither restates its code or
// its source representation. That reuse is the whole benefit.
//
// ⭐ ANY CONCEPT MAY CARRY A `code is`, AND EVERY ONE THAT DOES IS ANSWERABLE.
// A local code IS the answer slot — that is all it is, at every level. A concept
// with a code AND a derivation simply has two arms, and answering is one more way
// to establish it alongside computing it.
//
// ⚠ There is NO rule that a history is answerable and a reduction is not. An
// earlier version of this file said exactly that ("a selection is not a thing you
// answer") and it was wrong. `Greatest Weight` below carries a code precisely to
// keep that mistake from being re-derived from the file's shape: it reads a
// little odd for a measurement — asserting "the greatest weight is 200" — and it
// is entirely natural one layer up, where you may assert obesity, OR give a BMI,
// OR give a height and a weight. Same mechanism, different naturalness. The
// language does not legislate which; the AUTHOR chooses where to offer answers.
//
// ⚠ And nothing here shares a code. Two ways to answer come from TWO CODED
// CONCEPTS in one dependency chain, each with its own code, both surfaced when
// the question walk follows that chain. Sharing a code is not needed for it.
// ===========================================================================
library "Obesity Coverage (Layered)".

terminology "Obese VS":
- valueset is `http://example.org/vitals/ValueSet/obese`.
terminology "Clinical BMI VS":
- valueset is `http://example.org/vitals/ValueSet/bmi`.
terminology "Height VS":
- valueset is `http://example.org/vitals/ValueSet/height`.
terminology "Weight VS":
- valueset is `http://example.org/vitals/ValueSet/weight`.

// ── LAYER 1 — the HISTORIES ────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Coded, so answerable: answering adds a record, and the record is in the
// history by construction.

concept "Height Records":
- shape is RecordSet.
- type is Observation.
- value type is Quantity.
- code is `height`.
- source representation:
  - type is Observation.
  - coded from "Height VS".

concept "Weight Records":
- shape is RecordSet.
- type is Observation.
- value type is Quantity.
- code is `weight`.
- source representation:
  - type is Observation.
  - coded from "Weight VS".

// ── LAYER 2 — the NAMED REDUCTIONS ─────────────────────────────────────────
// Each names ONE reduction of ONE history. ⭐ Two reductions over `Weight
// Records` is the point of the layer. Whether one carries a code is the author's
// call, not the layer's: `Greatest Weight` does, the other two do not.

concept "Most Recent Height":
- shape is Record.
- type is Observation.
- value type is Quantity.
- definition is most recent "Height Records".

concept "Most Recent Weight":
- shape is Record.
- type is Observation.
- value type is Quantity.
- definition is most recent "Weight Records".

// ⭐ CODED ON PURPOSE. An aggregation is answerable like anything else — this is
// the file's standing refutation of "a reduction is not a thing you answer".
concept "Greatest Weight":
- shape is Record.
- type is Observation.
- value type is Quantity.
- code is `greatest-weight`.
- definition is highest "Weight Records".

// ── LAYER 3 — the DERIVED DETERMINATIONS ───────────────────────────────────
// Each has one value reached three ways — asserted, recorded, derived — and
// `, then most recent this` reduces over the concept's OWN records, which hold
// ALL THREE: the asserted arm (`code is`), the recorded arm (`source
// representation`), and the derived candidate the stage before it produced.
// ⚠ Naming only the first two reads the derivation as something OUTSIDE `this`,
// which is exactly backwards — `, then` is what puts its result inside. ⚠ Not layered ONLY because nothing needs a second reduction of them; if
// something did, they would layer like the histories.

// Derived from the measurement selections; also recordable and assertable.
concept "BMI":
- shape is Record.
- type is Observation.
- value type is Quantity.
- code is `bmi`.
- definition is body mass index of "Most Recent Weight" and "Most Recent Height", then most recent this.
- source representation:
  - type is Observation.
  - coded from "Clinical BMI VS".

// The coverage criterion. Asserted by a clinician, recorded as a Condition, or
// derived from BMI — whichever is newest wins.
concept "Obese":
- shape is Record.
- type is Observation.
- value type is boolean.
- code is `obese`.
- definition is "BMI" at least 30 'kg/m2', then most recent this.
- source representation:
  - type is Condition.
  - coded from "Obese VS".
  - value projection is exists this.

activity "Approve Bariatric Surgery":
- request CPGCommunicationRequest.
- with `APPROVED`.

activity "Deny Bariatric Surgery":
- request CPGCommunicationRequest.
- with `NOT APPROVED`.

decision "Bariatric Surgery Coverage":
first:
- when "Obese" then recommend activity "Approve Bariatric Surgery".
- otherwise then recommend activity "Deny Bariatric Surgery".
